Privilege and Work Product Protection
Not all materials that are highly relevant and easily retrieved are within the scope of discovery. In this section, we will discuss the two most common bases for withholding relevant, proportional materials from discovery: attorney-client privilege and work product protection.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON ATTORNEY-CLIENT PRIVILEGE, SODEXOMAGIC V. DREXEL UNIVERSITY, AND UPJOHN V. UNITED STATES
In the course of defining the scope of discovery, Rule 26(b)(1) requires not only that the materials in question be relevant, but that they be nonprivileged. The Rules do not define what materials are privileged, however. Rather, the Federal Rules of Evidence explicitly leave the development of privilege law to the continued evolution of the common law.

The most familiar and frequently invoked privilege is the attorney-client privilege. (Other privileges, such as spousal privileges and physician-patient privileges, protect other categories of confidential communications, but we will focus solely on attorney-client privilege here.) The attorney-client privilege is an ancient concept in the common law. It provides an absolute shield from discovery for certain communications, no matter how relevant, and no matter how great the need of the requesting party. In order for materials or testimony to be subject to the attorney-client privilege, there are four, generally recognized elements that must be met:

First, the communication involves a client or someone seeking to be a client.

Second, the communication was made to a lawyer (or a lawyer’s subordinate) acting in her capacity as a lawyer.

Third, the communication was confidential and made in the course of seeking legal advice and assistance.

Fourth, the privilege has been claimed and not waived.

As its fourth element reveals (and as the term “privilege” implies), the attorney-client privilege does not operate automatically—it must be invoked
