and can be relinquished. But if a party makes a valid claim of attorney-client privilege, privileged materials are categorically undiscoverable. The privilege belongs to the client, and the client may invoke it to shield their communications from discovery.

Although centuries old, the boundaries of the attorney-client privilege remain elusive to many attorneys. Generations of junior attorneys at law firms have had the experience of sorting through hundreds or thousands of documents to separate those that are privileged from those that are discoverable.

Which documents are subject to the attorney-client privilege? The easy—but wrong—answer is to slap the label “attorney-client privileged” on anything with a lawyer’s name on it. In our next case, SodexoMAGIC v. Drexel University, a judge offers some simple and concrete guidance for determining when a communication is privileged and when it isn’t.

And although ancient, the attorney-client privilege continues to pose new and difficult questions. For example, whether a communication is privileged depends on whether it is between an attorney and her client. Who is the “client”? When the client is an individual, answering this question is usually easy, but what if the client is a corporation or another type of large organization? The court in SodexoMAGIC addresses the simplest version of this scenario: when the president or vice president of a company seeks legal advice from corporate counsel (whether in-house counsel or an outside law firm), this is an attorney-client communication. But what about communications involving lower-level employees rather than the CEO? What about communications among hundreds of employees, rather than just one or two? Our second case, Upjohn v. United States, speaks to these questions.

